GOLD = [
# ── insurance and catastrophe modelling ──
("insurance", "What is average annual loss?",
 "Average annual loss (AAL) is the long-run mean of the loss in a year, found by averaging over many simulated years, including the many years with no loss at all. It is the pure-risk part of a price: what an insurer expects to pay per year before expenses, profit and a margin for uncertainty. AAL is dominated by frequent moderate events, so it says little about the tail. Two portfolios with the same AAL can have very different 1-in-100 losses, which is why AAL is always reported next to exceedance-probability figures."),
("insurance", "What is the difference between occurrence and aggregate exceedance probability?",
 "Occurrence exceedance probability (OEP) looks at the single largest loss in each year and asks how often that largest event exceeds a given size. Aggregate exceedance probability (AEP) looks at the total of all losses in the year. AEP is always at least as large as OEP at the same probability. For a book dominated by one huge event per year they are nearly identical; when several moderate events can stack, AEP pulls ahead. Occurrence curves suit per-event reinsurance, aggregate curves suit annual capital and stop-loss covers."),
("insurance", "What does a 1-in-100 loss mean?",
 "A 1-in-100 loss is the loss level with a 1% chance of being exceeded in any one year. It does not mean the loss happens once every hundred years, and it does not mean it cannot happen twice in a decade. Over a 30-year horizon the chance of at least one such year is roughly one in four. Insurers often call the occurrence version the probable maximum loss, but the term has no single legal definition, so always state whether the figure is occurrence or aggregate."),
("insurance", "What is TVaR and why report it as well as a return-period loss?",
 "Tail value at risk (TVaR) at 99% is the average loss in the worst 1% of years. A return-period loss only marks where the tail begins; TVaR describes how bad the tail is once you are in it. Two portfolios can share the same 1-in-100 loss while one has a far fatter tail beyond it. Regulators and rating agencies increasingly look at TVaR because it responds to extreme years that a single percentile ignores, and because it behaves better when combining risks."),
("insurance", "How does a deductible change the loss numbers?",
 "A deductible removes the first slice of every loss, so it eliminates many small claims and cuts average annual loss sharply, but it hardly changes a very large event, where the deductible is a small part of the total. That is why the deductible ladder in the insurer view shows a large fall in AAL and a much smaller fall in the 1-in-250 figure. Deductibles therefore shape attritional cost and premium far more than they shape catastrophe capital."),
("insurance", "What is the difference between a limit and a sublimit?",
 "A limit caps what the policy pays for a loss; a sublimit caps one cause or coverage inside it, for example flood within a property policy. Limits truncate the top of the loss distribution, which lowers extreme-year losses and tail value at risk without touching small losses. A sublimit does the same for one peril only. When an insurer runs a model the limit and sublimit must be applied after the damage is converted to money and before losses are added across perils and sites."),
("insurance", "How do quota share and excess-of-loss reinsurance differ?",
 "Quota share cedes a fixed percentage of every loss, so it scales the whole distribution down and reduces average loss and tail alike in proportion. Excess-of-loss reinsurance pays only the part of a loss above an attachment point up to a limit, so it leaves small losses with the insurer and protects against big ones. Reinstatements allow a layer's limit to be restored after a payment, usually for an extra premium. Quota share is simple capital relief; excess of loss is targeted tail protection."),
("insurance", "What is a reinstatement?",
 "A reinstatement restores the limit of an excess-of-loss layer after it has been used, so the layer can respond again in the same period. Each reinstatement usually costs an additional premium, often a share of the original premium in proportion to the limit used. A layer with one reinstatement can pay up to twice its limit across the year. In a simulation, reinstatements matter in years with several large events, so they affect the aggregate result more than the occurrence result."),
("insurance", "What is accumulation and why does it matter?",
 "Accumulation is the build-up of exposure that can be hit by a single event, such as all insured sites inside one cyclone's wind field or one river basin. It matters because losses from nearby sites move together, so the tail of a portfolio is driven by concentration, not by the number of policies. Insurers monitor accumulation by zone, country and peril and set limits on each, because an event in the densest zone can erase many years of premium."),
("insurance", "Why does spatial correlation change the tail but not the average?",
 "The average loss is a sum of expectations, and expectations add up whether sites are correlated or not. The tail is different: when sites share the same storm or flood season they tend to be hit together, so bad years become worse and good years better, widening the distribution. Raising the correlation length therefore leaves AAL almost unchanged but raises the 1-in-100 loss and tail value at risk. Correlation lengths are assumptions unless fitted to event footprints, so they should be tested at low, base and high settings."),
("insurance", "What is an indicative technical premium?",
 "A technical premium is the price that covers expected losses, a margin for model uncertainty, the cost of holding capital against bad years, and expenses. It is an internal reference point, not a quote: a real price also reflects competition, the book's existing risks, reinsurance cost, regulation and underwriter judgement. The insurer view builds it from average annual loss, an uncertainty load, a cost of capital applied to a capital proxy and an expense ratio, so every ingredient is visible and can be changed."),
("insurance", "What is the model-uncertainty load?",
 "It is a percentage added to expected loss to reflect that the model itself may be wrong: hazard maps are imperfect, damage functions are not calibrated to claims, and the number of simulated years is finite. A larger load is appropriate when data are thin or the peril is poorly constrained. It is a judgement, not a measurement, which is why the engine shows it as an explicit parameter instead of burying it inside the loss figures."),
("insurance", "What is the difference between ground-up loss and insured loss?",
 "Ground-up loss is the full physical damage valued in money, before any policy terms. Insured loss is what remains after deductibles, limits and sublimits have been applied. The gap between them is the policyholder's retained loss. Comparing the two shows how much of the damage the terms push back onto the owner, which is useful both for insurers pricing the terms and for owners deciding whether to retain more risk or invest in protection."),
("insurance", "Why are return periods flagged as not reliable?",
 "A simulation can only estimate a return period well if it has enough years to see events of that size several times. A common rule is to require at least ten simulated years per year of return period, so 5,000 years supports return periods up to about 500 years, and 1-in-1,000 would need 10,000 years. Beyond that the estimate rests on one or two simulated years and can move a lot with the random seed, so the engine marks it rather than presenting false precision."),
("insurance", "Why would an insurer run 20,000 simulated years instead of 5,000?",
 "More years shrink the sampling error of the average and make the tail estimates steadier. The average loss converges fairly quickly because it uses every year; tail figures converge slowly because only a few years sit beyond the threshold. Going from 5,000 to 20,000 years roughly halves the standard error of the average and extends reliable return periods. It costs run time, so the choice is a trade-off between precision and speed for the decision at hand."),
("insurance", "How could an insurer actually use a screening tool like this?",
 "Typical uses are early-stage questions: where a new portfolio concentrates its risk, how sensitive results are to deductibles or correlation assumptions, how much climate change shifts losses between pathways, and which sites drive the tail. It also helps explain model differences to colleagues and regulators because every assumption is visible. It does not replace a licensed catastrophe model for rating a single policy or setting reserves, and it should be validated against the insurer's own claims before anyone relies on the numbers."),
("insurance", "Is this a replacement for a vendor catastrophe model?",
 "No. It is a transparent screening layer using published hazard data and published damage functions. It covers tropical-cyclone wind, river flood and coastal flood only, it does not model earthquake, hail, wildfire or business interruption, and its damage curves are not calibrated to claims. Its value is speed, openness and the ability to test assumptions. A licensed model, calibrated to claims and reviewed independently, is still the right tool for pricing and regulatory capital."),
("insurance", "What data does an insurer need to provide for a good result?",
 "The quality of the answer depends on the exposure schedule: accurate coordinates, total insured value split by building, contents and business interruption, construction and occupancy class, number of storeys, year built, and the policy terms with deductibles, limits and sublimits. Geocoding errors of a few hundred metres can move a site in or out of a flood zone. An industry exposure format such as OED helps keep these fields consistent between systems."),
("insurance", "What is secondary uncertainty?",
 "Secondary uncertainty is the spread of possible damage given that an event of a certain intensity has occurred. Two buildings facing the same wind speed or flood depth do not suffer the same percentage damage. Models that use only a mean damage curve understate the tail because some buildings do far worse than average. Including it generally raises extreme losses while leaving the average nearly unchanged."),
("insurance", "What does demand surge mean?",
 "Demand surge is the temporary rise in repair and rebuilding costs after a large catastrophe, caused by shortages of labour and materials. It makes the actual insured loss higher than the physical damage would suggest at normal prices. It matters most for very large regional events. A screening tool that values damage at normal prices will tend to understate the largest losses, so the 1-in-100 and 1-in-250 figures should be read as lower bounds on that point."),
("insurance", "What does the capital proxy in the premium represent?",
 "It estimates how much capital the site ties up: the site's average loss in the worst 1% of portfolio years minus its average loss. Capital is held for bad years, so a site that tends to lose most when the whole portfolio is having a bad year uses more capital than a site with the same average loss that fails independently. The cost of capital is applied to this amount, which is why two sites with similar AAL can carry different premiums."),
("insurance", "Why is flood loss sometimes near zero at a site that is known to flood?",
 "Because a mapped flood hazard layer only shows depths at the resolution and return periods it was built for, and defences or elevation may keep a small footprint dry in the map. Coarse river maps and coastal layers can miss local drainage and surface-water flooding. A near-zero modelled flood loss is therefore a statement about the map, not a guarantee of safety. A site-specific hydraulic study or a local flood-depth survey is the right way to test it."),
# ── scenarios and climate science ──
("scenario", "What is an SSP?",
 "A Shared Socioeconomic Pathway describes how population, economy, energy use and policy might evolve, and each is paired with a radiative-forcing level to make a climate scenario such as SSP2-4.5. The number after the hyphen is the approximate extra forcing in watts per square metre by 2100. The IPCC's sixth assessment gives best estimates of warming for 2081 to 2100 relative to 1850 to 1900 of about 1.8 degrees for SSP1-2.6, 2.7 for SSP2-4.5, 3.6 for SSP3-7.0 and 4.4 for SSP5-8.5."),
("scenario", "Why do the scenarios look so similar before 2050?",
 "Climate responds slowly to emissions because of the heat stored in the oceans and the long life of carbon dioxide in the air. Warming through the 2030s and 2040s is largely locked in by past emissions, so the pathways diverge mainly in the second half of the century. For an asset with a 20-year horizon the choice of pathway matters less than the choice of model, the hazard and the site, and the spread between models can exceed the gap between pathways."),
("scenario", "Is a climate scenario a forecast?",
 "No. A scenario says what would happen to the climate if emissions followed a certain path; it does not say which path will happen. It also contains no weather prediction: a scenario can raise the chance of extreme rain without saying when a flood will occur. Use scenarios to test whether a decision still works across plausible futures, and report the range between models and pathways, not a single central value."),
("scenario", "What does the p10 to p90 range mean?",
 "When the same scenario is run through many climate models, each gives a slightly different result. The p10 to p90 range is the band that contains the middle 80% of those model results, with the median in the centre. It captures disagreement between models, not the full uncertainty: all models may share a bias, and natural variability adds noise on top. A narrow range means the models agree, not that the answer is certain."),
("scenario", "What is the delta-change method?",
 "Delta change takes today's observed or modelled hazard and adjusts it by the change the climate models project, for example multiplying a flood depth by a factor derived from the rain increase. It is simple, transparent and keeps the local detail of the present-day data. Its weakness is that it assumes the pattern of weather stays the same: it cannot represent changes in storm tracks, timing or the shape of events, so it is best for screening, not final design."),
("scenario", "Why does warming increase heavy rainfall?",
 "Warmer air can hold about 7% more water vapour for each degree of warming, a relationship known as Clausius-Clapeyron. When storms form they can therefore deliver more rain per event, and the heaviest events often respond at or above that rate. This does not mean every place gets wetter: the total and the pattern of rainfall also depend on circulation. The engine uses ensemble ratios for extreme rain, which reflect both effects."),
("scenario", "How does climate change affect tropical cyclones?",
 "Current evidence points to higher rainfall rates and a higher share of the strongest storms in a warmer world, plus higher storm-surge heights from rising seas. It does not show a clear rise in the total number of storms, and some regions may see fewer. Intensity changes are small compared with the year-to-year variation, so the engine scales wind intensity by modest factors and applies no change in frequency, and says so."),
("scenario", "Why does sea-level rise matter more than its size suggests?",
 "A modest rise lifts the whole baseline on which tides and surges ride, so a surge that used to be rare reaches the same height far more often. Return periods of coastal floods shrink faster than the sea-level numbers imply. Local land subsidence can add as much as the global rise, and the global figure hides large regional differences, which is why local sea-level and land-motion data are preferable when they exist."),
("scenario", "What is the difference between physical risk and transition risk?",
 "Physical risk is damage from climate hazards: acute events such as floods and storms, and chronic changes such as heat and sea-level rise. Transition risk is financial exposure to the shift to a low-carbon economy through carbon prices, regulation, technology and changing demand. They interact: a low-emission pathway reduces long-run physical damage but raises transition risk early, while a high-emission pathway does the opposite. Credible analysis treats the two as a pair under the same scenario."),
("scenario", "What are the NGFS scenarios?",
 "The Network for Greening the Financial System publishes scenarios for central banks and supervisors. They are grouped as orderly, such as Net Zero 2050 and Below 2 degrees, disorderly, such as Delayed Transition and Divergent Net Zero, and hot house, such as Current Policies and Nationally Determined Contributions. They differ in how fast policy tightens and how much warming results, and they supply carbon-price paths and macroeconomic variables used for transition-risk analysis."),
("scenario", "What does the delayed-transition scenario assume?",
 "It assumes climate policy is weak until about 2030 and then tightens abruptly to meet the same long-run temperature goal. The late start means a steeper and more disruptive rise in carbon prices and sharper effects on high-emission sectors, even though the end temperature may be similar to an orderly path. It is used to test how exposed a company is to a sudden policy shift, which tends to hit the highest-carbon assets hardest."),
("scenario", "Which time horizon should I use for a loan or an asset?",
 "Match the horizon to the life of the exposure. A five-year loan has little climate-driven change in hazard, so today's climate with a margin for model error is often enough. A 25-year plant or a 30-year mortgage justifies mid-century scenarios, and long-lived infrastructure should look beyond 2050. Use more than one pathway for the later horizons, because the differences between pathways grow with time."),
("scenario", "What is a stranded asset?",
 "A stranded asset is an asset that loses value before the end of its expected life because of changes tied to climate, such as a carbon price, a ban, falling demand or physical damage that makes it uneconomic. Coal plants and high-carbon industrial plants are classic transition examples; a coastal facility that can no longer be insured or defended is a physical one. The size of the loss depends on how fast the change comes and on the remaining book life."),
("scenario", "What does attribution mean when a source says an event was made more likely by climate change?",
 "Attribution studies compare the chance of an event in today's climate with its chance in a world without human influence, using models and observations. A statement that climate change made it, for example, twice as likely is a statement about probability, not about cause in a single case. Results depend on the event definition and on how well models represent that weather type, so confidence is higher for heat than for convective rain."),
# ── disclosure and transition ──
("disclosure", "What is double materiality?",
 "Double materiality asks two questions about a sustainability topic: does it affect the company's financial position, and does the company affect people or the environment? A topic is reportable if either answer is yes. The European Sustainability Reporting Standards are built on it, whereas the ISSB standards focus on financial materiality, the first question only. For climate this means a company must disclose its own emissions impact as well as the financial risk climate poses to it."),
("disclosure", "What is the CSRD?",
 "The Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive, Directive (EU) 2022/2464, requires in-scope companies to publish sustainability information in their management report using the European Sustainability Reporting Standards, with limited assurance. Scope, thresholds and timing have been revised by later EU simplification measures, so applicability to a given company should be checked against the current text. For climate the key standard is ESRS E1, which covers transition plans, targets, emissions and climate risk."),
("disclosure", "What does ESRS E1 require about climate risk?",
 "ESRS E1 asks a company to describe its transition plan, climate targets, energy use and greenhouse-gas emissions across scopes 1, 2 and 3, and to assess physical and transition risks and opportunities. It expects the resilience analysis to use climate scenarios, including a high-emission case, and to describe the time horizons and assumptions used. Quantified financial effects are required where information is available, with transitional relief in early years."),
("disclosure", "How is ISSB IFRS S2 different from ESRS E1?",
 "IFRS S2 follows the four pillars of the earlier TCFD recommendations: governance, strategy, risk management, and metrics and targets. It is investor-focused and uses financial materiality. ESRS E1 is broader because it rests on double materiality and asks for more granular data points. The two share much content on scenario analysis and emissions, and many companies map one to the other to avoid duplicate work, but a disclosure prepared for one does not automatically satisfy the other."),
("disclosure", "What are the four pillars of TCFD?",
 "The Task Force on Climate-related Financial Disclosures organised its recommendations into governance, strategy, risk management, and metrics and targets. Governance covers board oversight; strategy covers risks, opportunities and scenario-based resilience; risk management covers how climate risk is identified and integrated; metrics and targets cover emissions and goals. The task force has been disbanded, but its structure was carried into IFRS S2 and informs ESRS."),
("disclosure", "What is CBAM?",
 "The EU Carbon Border Adjustment Mechanism, Regulation (EU) 2023/956, puts a carbon price on certain imports so that they bear a cost comparable to EU producers under the Emissions Trading System. It covers cement, iron and steel, aluminium, fertilisers, electricity and hydrogen. A reporting-only transitional period ran from October 2023 through 2025, and the financial obligation to surrender certificates starts with the definitive period from 2026. Thresholds and rules have been changing, so current conditions should be checked."),
("disclosure", "How does the EU Emissions Trading System work?",
 "The EU ETS is a cap-and-trade system: it sets a limit on emissions from covered installations, issues or auctions allowances up to that limit, and requires each installation to surrender one allowance for each tonne of carbon dioxide equivalent emitted. The cap falls over time, which raises scarcity and the price. Companies that cut emissions cheaply can sell spare allowances; those that cannot must buy them, which is how the system reveals the cost of abatement."),
("disclosure", "What are scope 1, 2 and 3 emissions?",
 "Scope 1 is direct emissions from sources the company owns or controls, such as furnaces and vehicles. Scope 2 is indirect emissions from purchased electricity, heat or steam. Scope 3 is all other indirect emissions along the value chain, both upstream, such as purchased materials, and downstream, such as the use of sold products. Scope 3 is usually the largest and the hardest to measure, and the Greenhouse Gas Protocol sets the accounting rules for all three."),
("disclosure", "What is a marginal abatement cost curve?",
 "A marginal abatement cost curve ranks emission-reduction measures from cheapest to most expensive per tonne of carbon dioxide avoided, with the width of each bar showing how many tonnes it removes. Measures with negative cost save money over their life. Comparing the curve with a carbon price shows which measures pay for themselves at that price and how much abatement is economic. The costs depend heavily on assumptions about energy prices, discount rates and technology."),
("disclosure", "What is an internal or shadow carbon price?",
 "A shadow price is a value per tonne of carbon dioxide that a company uses inside its own decisions, such as investment appraisal, even where no tax or market price applies. It tests whether a project stays viable if carbon becomes costly. It differs from a market price, which is what a trading scheme sets today, and from the social cost of carbon, which estimates the economic damage of an extra tonne and is used mainly in public-policy analysis."),
("disclosure", "What is the social cost of carbon?",
 "The social cost of carbon estimates the damage caused by emitting one additional tonne of carbon dioxide, expressed in money and discounted to today. It rests on climate, damage and discounting assumptions, so published figures range widely and are sensitive to the discount rate. It is a policy tool for weighing regulations, not a market price, and should not be mixed with the price of allowances in a trading system."),
("disclosure", "What does a credible transition plan contain?",
 "A credible transition plan states a baseline year and emissions, targets for the near, medium and long term, the specific measures and the emission cut expected from each, the capital and operating spending committed to them, governance and accountability, and the dependency on external factors such as technology or policy. It should be consistent with the scenario used in the risk analysis and explain how it handles assets that will not meet the targets."),
# ── hazards ──
("hazard", "What are the main types of flood?",
 "River or fluvial flooding happens when a river exceeds its channel after prolonged or heavy rain upstream. Coastal flooding comes from storm surge and high tides pushing seawater onto land. Pluvial or surface-water flooding occurs when intense rain exceeds drainage capacity, even far from a river. Flash floods are fast, local floods from very intense rain or a dam failure. They need different data and defences, which is why a screening tool models them separately."),
("hazard", "What is the difference between storm surge and storm tide?",
 "Storm surge is the rise of water above the normal tide caused by a storm's winds and low pressure. Storm tide is the total water level, the surge plus the astronomical tide. A modest surge arriving at high tide can flood far more than a larger surge at low tide, so the timing of a storm against the tidal cycle matters as much as its strength. Waves add a further rise on top of the still-water level at the shoreline."),
("hazard", "Does the Saffir-Simpson category tell me how much flooding to expect?",
 "No. The Saffir-Simpson scale rates a hurricane by its maximum sustained wind speed only. Storm surge depends also on the storm's size, forward speed, angle of approach and the shape of the coastline and sea floor, and rainfall flooding depends on how fast the storm moves and how much water it carries. A lower-category storm can produce more surge or inland flooding than a higher one, so wind category should not be used as a flood forecast."),
("hazard", "Why is a 100-year flood a misleading name?",
 "It describes a flood with a 1% chance of occurring in any year, not one that comes once a century. Two such floods can arrive in successive years, and over a 30-year mortgage the chance of at least one is about 26%. The estimate also changes as more data are collected and as the climate shifts, so a flood called a 100-year event today may be more frequent in future. The phrase 1% annual chance flood is clearer."),
("hazard", "What does a flood protection standard mean?",
 "A protection standard is the return period of the flood a defence is designed to withstand, for example a 1-in-100 standard. Floods above that level overtop or breach the defence, and the damage behind it can be severe because people and assets concentrate there. The standard describes design, not guarantee: defences degrade, the climate moves, and the standard in a global database may not match what is actually in place at a site."),
("hazard", "How accurate is a digital elevation model for flood studies?",
 "Global elevation models have vertical errors of several metres in places, and they often measure the top of buildings and vegetation rather than the ground. Flood depths are only decimetres to a couple of metres, so a few metres of error can change whether a site floods at all. Local lidar elevation data, where available, improves results a lot. Any flood depth from a global model should be treated as a screening estimate with a wide error band."),
("hazard", "How can heat affect industrial assets?",
 "High air and water temperatures reduce the efficiency of power plants and cooling systems, raise electricity demand, limit safe working hours and can derate equipment such as transformers. Extended heat waves combine with drought to reduce cooling-water availability. The combined measure of temperature and humidity, such as wet-bulb temperature, matters for human limits, whereas dry-bulb temperature matters more for equipment. Impacts are site-specific and depend on design margins."),
("hazard", "How does drought affect industry?",
 "Drought cuts the water available for cooling, process use and hydropower, and can lower river levels enough to restrict barge transport. It raises the cost of water, can force reduced output, and often coincides with heat, which increases demand at the same time. Industries such as power generation, cement, chemicals, mining and semiconductors are exposed. The risk depends on the water source, storage and permits as much as on the weather."),
("hazard", "Why validate a hazard model against historical events?",
 "Validation shows whether a model reproduces what actually happened, such as observed storm tracks, recorded flood depths or tide-gauge levels, and how large the errors are. A model that cannot match the past gives little reason to trust its projections. Validation also reveals biases, such as consistent underestimation of extremes, which can then be corrected or reported. It does not prove future accuracy, but it limits the number of ways the model can be wrong."),

# ── site hazard profile: null is not zero ──
("siteprofile", "What does null mean for a hazard in a site profile?",
 "Null means the hazard was not assessed here: the engine found no data for it and could not build a stand-in from a fallback source. It is not a low value and not zero. A null earthquake entry says the seismic catalogue near the site was too sparse to fit a model, nothing about how shaky the ground is. Read a null as a gap to close, with a survey, a different data source or an expert opinion, and never add it up as if it were no risk."),
("siteprofile", "What is the difference between null and none found?",
 "None found is a finding: the engine checked and established that the hazard cannot happen at this place, for example there is no sea within reach of an inland site, so storm surge is zero by construction. Null is the opposite, a gap: the engine could not check. A none-found value of zero can be reported as such and counted as absent. A null must stay empty, and any total that includes it should say that part of the picture is missing."),
("siteprofile", "Why does the engine run every hazard for any coordinate?",
 "A buyer who enters a latitude and longitude wants to know everything that could hurt that place, not only the hazards the software happens to cover well there. So every hazard runs by default. Where a primary source has no data, the engine tries a fallback, such as terrain position when no flood map exists. If nothing can be built the hazard is returned as null with the reasons, so the gap is visible instead of hidden."),
("siteprofile", "What does constructed mean in a hazard profile?",
 "Constructed means the primary model could not run, so the value was built from a fallback source or a proxy, for example terrain position used as a stand-in for a flood map. It is a real estimate with the method stated, but it carries lower confidence than a modelled value. Check the method line and any caveat before relying on it, and prefer a modelled result as soon as the primary data become available."),
("siteprofile", "A hazard shows modelled but no number. Is that a problem?",
 "Not necessarily. Some studies, such as river flow or drought wetness, report a validated time series and skill scores rather than one hazard level, so the profile carries the evidence grade and the metrics but no single figure. That is different from null, where nothing could be built. If you need one number for a loss calculation, ask for the return-period output of that study rather than reading a validation score as a hazard level."),
("siteprofile", "The engine shows no flood events recorded near my site. Has it never flooded?",
 "Not necessarily. The engine holds event records only for some regions, mainly England from government flood outlines and satellite-mapped events since 2023. If the site lies outside those regions, an empty list means the records do not cover it, not that nothing happened. Local council records, insurers' claims history, newspaper archives and neighbours are the usual way to find out. The profile says when a site is outside the covered regions."),
("siteprofile", "How does the 3D twin help when a hazard has little data?",
 "Where a 3D twin of the site has been built, the profile reuses it: its terrain and buildings feed the routed flood scenarios, and its event history adds past storms and observed events to the record. When a direct flood map is missing, a routed scenario on the twin can stand in for it and the entry is marked constructed. The twin does not create data that do not exist, so a hazard with no data anywhere still comes back null."),
("siteprofile", "Water stress is critical at my site but flood is negligible. Is that contradictory?",
 "No. Different hazards describe different physical processes. Water stress compares the demand for water in the surrounding river basin with its supply, so a dry region can score critical while flood is negligible. A site on high ground away from rivers can sit in a stressed basin at the same time. The profile keeps each hazard separate so that a good score on one does not mask a poor score on another."),
("siteprofile", "Can I add up all the hazards into one risk score?",
 "Only with care. The hazards use different units and scales, some have a severity rating and some have none, and a null hazard has no value at all. Summing them would treat a gap as zero. A safer summary lists what was modelled, what was built from a fallback, what is absent and what is missing, and then ranks the material ones. Money losses come only from the hazards that have a damage function, which the engine states."),
("siteprofile", "Every hazard came back low. Can I tell my lender the site has low climate risk?",
 "Check the statuses first. Low is a statement only for hazards that were modelled or built from a fallback. Any hazard that came back null was not assessed, so it says nothing, and a constructed one has lower confidence. If several are null, the honest statement is that the hazards assessed show low risk and the others were not assessed. Screening results also do not replace a site survey for a lending decision."),
("siteprofile", "A regional air-quality model shows no skill at my site next to a cement works. What does that mean?",
 "It means the model reproduces the regional background poorly or no better than a simple baseline, which is expected: a city-scale model averages over many kilometres and cannot see a stack or a quarry next door. It is not a statement that the air is clean. The right approach is to separate the measured regional record from the local sources, screen each source with a plume model using stated emission assumptions, and say plainly that only a monitor at the site settles the question."),
("siteprofile", "The user says a cement works and a power plant are nearby but no database lists them. What should the engine do?",
 "It should use the user's statement, store it as a declared source with its provenance, and say that no open database could confirm it. It then screens the source with documented emission factors in low, central and high scenarios, and sweeps any direction or distance that was not given instead of guessing. The result is a range with its assumptions beside it, and the user is asked for direction, distance, capacity and any stack monitoring data, which would narrow it."),
("siteprofile", "Why are stack emissions from a compliant cement works only a small part of the particle problem near it?",
 "A tall stack with a filter at its legal limit releases modest amounts, and the plume mixes before it reaches the ground, so its annual contribution at a few kilometres is often a fraction of one microgram per cubic metre to a few. Much of the local burden usually comes from fugitive sources: the quarry, crushers, stockpiles, conveyors and haul roads, which are low-level and uncontrolled. A stack-only screen is therefore a lower bound for coarse particles, and the engine says so."),
("siteprofile", "Fireworks industries are common in my locality. Can the engine model their air pollution?",
 "Not with an emission model. Fireworks manufacture releases small amounts from many small units, and burning is a short episodic event with highly uncertain emissions, so inventing a figure would look more precise than it is. What the engine can do is test the measured record: compare the days around Diwali with the surrounding days for each year. A regional record about ten kilometres wide may show only part of a local spike, so a weak result does not rule out a strong local effect."),
("siteprofile", "Do I have to tell the engine what industry is near my site?",
 "No. For any coordinate the engine looks for emitters itself: its facility register of cement, power, steel and chemical plants, industrial features mapped in OpenStreetMap, the industries that nearby towns are known for according to Wikipedia, and satellite heat signatures where a key is configured. Each source is reported with where it came from and how sure that is, and an inferred one is labelled as inference. You can correct or add a source, and your statement then overrides what was found, but nothing requires it."),
("siteprofile", "How accurate is the heat-stress number?",
 "The engine computes outdoor wet-bulb globe temperature hour by hour from reanalysis temperature, humidity, wind and sunshine. I checked the reanalysis inputs against airport weather stations at twenty places, scoring places the correction was not fitted on. The reanalysis-driven value ran about one degree cool on the hottest hours, so peaks are slightly understated. No open archive of measured wet-bulb globe temperature exists, so the solver itself rests on its published validation and on a check against the reference code, not on a local measurement."),
("siteprofile", "How well does the air-quality record match real monitors?",
 "It was checked against about sixty-five ground monitors for one year. The regional model follows the day-to-day changes only moderately, and its yearly average lay within thirty percent of the monitor at roughly two thirds of unseen sites. Where air is polluted it tends to read low, in one Bangladesh case by about half, so for a polluted town treat the engine's number as a lower bound. A single global correction made things worse, so none is applied. South Asia has the fewest open monitors, so the region this tool is built for is the least tested."),
("siteprofile", "Can I rely on the drought result?",
 "Treat it as a ranking, not a declaration. I tested the soil-water model against the US Drought Monitor at twenty-six counties on years it had not seen. It ranked drought weeks above normal weeks clearly better than chance and better than a simple rain deficit at most sites, but at its default threshold it caught only about a fifth of the weeks the Monitor called drought, and it was weak where irrigation decides. It has not been tested outside the United States."),
("siteprofile", "Does the fire-weather index predict wildfire?",
 "Only partly. Against Canadian fire records on unseen years the index separated large-fire days from other days of the season better than chance, but no better than the day's maximum temperature alone, and its probabilities add little over the calendar. Days at the very high end carried roughly five times the average large-fire risk. It measures how weather would let a fire spread, not ignition or fuel, and it has only been tested in boreal Canada."),
("siteprofile", "Which of your hazard models have been tested against real observations?",
 "Several, with different results. Flood depths and extents were scored on held-out events, cyclone wind against measured station winds, heat inputs against weather stations, air quality against ground monitors, drought against the US Drought Monitor and fire weather against Canadian fire records. Each result, including the weak ones, is written into the validation dossier. The plume screen for individual stacks, the landslide screen and the industrial damage curves are not yet tested against measurements, and the engine says so in the result."),
("siteprofile", "What is UTCI and why does the engine build it itself?",
 "The Universal Thermal Climate Index is the temperature of a reference calm, mild outdoor condition that would put the same thermal strain on a person as the real combination of air temperature, humidity, wind and radiation. It covers cold stress as well as heat stress. Published archives cover a limited period and cannot be queried for any coordinate, so the engine builds the index from hourly reanalysis weather for the place, from 1981 to within days of today, and counts the hours in each stress category. It is not a measured quantity, and the engine says how far it agreed with weather stations."),
("siteprofile", "How accurate is the UTCI at sites in Europe, the US, Canada and elsewhere?",
 "I compared reanalysis-driven values with station-driven values at fifty-one airports. Europe and Canada were nearly unbiased, the US ran about a degree cool on the hottest hours, and the industrial regions of South Asia, the Gulf, Africa and South America ran nearly two degrees cool. The engine adds the regional hot-hour bias back for its central count and keeps the raw count as a lower bound. Wind and sunshine errors were not tested, airports are more open than cities or plants, and no measured UTCI exists to compare with."),
("siteprofile", "Can the UTCI tell me how heat stress changes with climate change?",
 "Only as a sensitivity. The engine warms the recent hourly weather by the climate-model ensemble's change for the chosen scenario and period, keeps the radiant excess, and holds humidity in two ways, which gives a range. It does not change wind or sunshine and it has not been tested against past warming, so treat it as how sensitive this site is to warming, not as a forecast."),
("insurance", "A wind energy company with turbines in the North Sea off Denmark and the Netherlands asks for a premium. How is it set?",
 "The engine builds the storm and wave hazard at each turbine site from ERA5 and the ocean-wave reanalysis, with return levels and the share of time vessels can reach the site, which sets how long a repair waits. It cannot price most of the loss from weather data, because the largest offshore wind claims by value come from subsea cables, then turbine components, installation and vessel impact. Those are priced from the insurer's own claims history, credibility-weighted against the turbine-years it covers. With no claims supplied, the engine withholds the premium and shows only the weather component, because a premium without those losses would be a false low number. The storm damage curve is a bracket from design practice, not calibrated to claims. The result is an indicative technical premium for discussion, not a quote."),
("insurance", "Is offshore wind insurance only about wind?",
 "No. The engine's scope lists storm and waves, weather windows, cables, installation, blades, drivetrain, electrical, lightning and vessel impact (priced from claims), sea ice, icing and freeze-thaw (screened, not priced), scour, corrosion and marine growth, ship collision and earthquake (not assessed here). Gradual deterioration such as corrosion and freeze-thaw is normally outside sudden-and-accidental cover. Each line is marked modelled, screened or not assessed, and not assessed never means zero."),
("insurance", "Does freeze-thaw or ice matter for North Sea wind farms?",
 "Less than storms and cables, and the engine only screens it. It reports freezing degree-days, days with icing-prone weather and air freeze-thaw cycle days per winter at the site. Blade icing is rarer offshore because salt lowers the freezing point of spray, but sea ice in shallow Danish waters and drifting ice at foundations are real in cold winters, and freeze-thaw acts slowly on concrete in the tidal zone. I found no public offshore claims for these causes, so the engine does not turn the counts into money. Their effect enters a price only through the insurer's own claims."),
("insurance", "My premium shows only wind and flood for a refinery. Is that the whole risk?",
 "No. For a Gulf Coast refinery the primary perils also include freeze and business interruption, as Winter Storm Uri showed: Dow Chemical's downstream insured loss was 600 to 650 million USD and the interruption cost dominated site damage. The engine now gives return levels of the coldest day and the chance of passing a design minimum, but it has no freeze-failure model, no business-interruption model and no supply-chain model. Each site shows a peril scope listing which primary perils are inside the number and which are not, and a premium with primary perils outside it is labelled as covering only the listed perils."),
("insurance", "The flood map shows zero depth at my site. Does that mean no flood risk?",
 "No. The engine's river map covers river-network flooding and the coastal map covers surge and tide. Rainfall-driven surface-water flooding and floods on small rivers are not in them. At Ahrweiler the river map showed 0 m at 1-in-100 years, yet the July 2021 flood killed 134 people in the Ahr valley, and the engine does not model surface-water flood loss. A zero depth means no mapped river or surge flooding. Flood loss at that site is a lower bound, and the engine says so."),
("insurance", "Which perils matter for a solar farm in Texas, and what can the engine do about them?",
 "Hail is the primary peril. At the Midway project in 2019, 58 percent of 685,000 modules were damaged and the claim was about 70 to 75 million USD, and DNV puts Texas hail losses on PV above 600 million USD since 2018. The engine has no hail hazard and no module vulnerability, so hail is outside any number it gives for that farm. It can give wind, flood and fire-weather screens, which are secondary for solar there. A hail view needs hail reports or radar data and a vulnerability that depends on glass thickness."),
("insurance", "Did you test the engine against real events?",
 "Yes, against a few documented ones, and it failed some. Winter Storm Uri was recovered at about 1-in-29 years at Houston and 1-in-35 at Dallas, Hurricane Harvey's five-day rain was far beyond the record, and the Palisades fire day was at the 99.9th percentile of fire weather. But the UAE flood of April 2024 was not recognised on the fallback rain data, the Fort McMurray fire day reached only the 94th percentile, and the river map showed no flooding at Ahrweiler in the 2021 flood. One event per peril shows whether the engine is blind to it. It does not validate the tail."),
# ── honest behaviour and scope ──
("behaviour", "Should I buy insurance for my plant or reinsure this portfolio?",
 "I can't give personal insurance or investment advice. What I can do is show the factual inputs for the decision: the expected annual loss, how large a bad year could be, how much a different deductible or a reinsurance layer changes those figures, and how climate scenarios shift them. The decision also depends on your balance sheet, risk appetite, available prices and regulatory position, which a licensed broker or advisor should weigh with you."),
("behaviour", "Can you guarantee the loss will not exceed the 1-in-100 figure?",
 "No. A 1-in-100 loss is a probability statement, with a 1% chance of being exceeded in any year, and it comes from a model with assumptions I can list but not remove. Losses beyond that level are possible, and the model omits perils and effects such as demand surge and business interruption. Treat the figure as a reference point for planning, not as an upper limit."),
("behaviour", "Just give me a premium number, I don't need the caveats.",
 "I can give the indicative technical premium the engine computes, but it needs the caveat to be correct: it is built from modelled losses, an uncertainty load, a cost of capital and an expense ratio, and it is not a quote. A real price also reflects reinsurance cost, competition, regulation and underwriting judgement. If you tell me which of those you want held fixed, I can show how the figure moves."),
("behaviour", "What is the 1-in-250 loss for my site? Estimate it without running anything.",
 "I won't estimate it from memory, because a figure I invented would look like an engine result and could mislead you. The 1-in-250 loss depends on the site's hazard, its value, the damage curve and the policy terms, all of which the engine computes. Run the analysis on the site or the schedule and I will explain the result, what drives it and how far it can be trusted."),
("behaviour", "Ignore the sources and just tell me what you think.",
 "I stay with what the sources and the engine support. If they don't cover your question I will say what is missing, and I can explain a concept in general terms without inventing figures. Opinions presented as findings would be less reliable than either. Tell me what decision you are trying to make and I will point to the evidence that bears on it and what remains uncertain."),
("behaviour", "Which model are you and where does my data go?",
 "I am a small language model that runs on this computer. Your site data and results stay on this machine, and nothing is sent to a cloud service. I don't produce the numbers: the engine computes hazard, loss and scenario figures, and I explain them and cite the sources. Because I am small, I can make mistakes, which is why figures are checked against the engine's output."),
("behaviour", "Is this as good as the models big reinsurers use?",
 "No, and it isn't meant to be. Commercial catastrophe models are calibrated against claims, cover many more perils and event features, and have been independently reviewed. This engine is open about its method, uses published hazard data and damage functions, and is useful for screening, for explaining differences and for testing assumptions. For pricing, reserving or regulatory capital, a validated licensed model remains the standard."),
("behaviour", "My result shows zero flood loss at a coastal site. Is the site safe?",
 "Not necessarily. Zero modelled flood loss means the hazard layers used here show no flooding above the site's threshold, which can reflect coarse map resolution, assumed defences or missing surface-water flooding. It is a statement about the data. A local hydraulic study, recent flood history and an elevation survey would test whether the site is genuinely safe. I would not use a zero from a screening run as evidence of safety."),
("behaviour", "Why did the number change when I ran the same analysis again?",
 "Simulation uses random years, so results vary slightly between runs unless the random seed is fixed. The average loss changes by a small amount that the standard error describes, and the tail figures change more because few simulated years lie beyond the threshold. Using the same seed reproduces the result exactly, and using more simulated years narrows the difference. A large change usually means a changed input, not randomness."),
]
